# Bandit

**Alignments.** Typically anarchist or evil, and the typical bandit does not
live by the Code of the New West. Robin Hood types, and those who victimise
only the Coalition, other bandits or evil forces, may be any alignment
including good.

Bandit, bandito, Raider, gangsman, highwayman, bushwhacker - whatever the
character calls himself, the typical bandit is a vagabond thug. Most are under
thirty-five, drifters born to peasants, and see robbery as both a career and a
way to be somebody. Ninety-eight percent are uneducated and fewer than one in a
hundred can read at all.

They are poor wilderness travellers, because they do not need to be: bandits
move in groups, pool their skills, and go from town to camp to village taking
what they need on the way. A working week might take in a picked pocket, a
sleeping drunk''s guns, a crooked hand of cards, a man shot for talking back, a
stolen horse, rustled cattle, a staged holdup and a fence for the proceeds.

The murderous ones get the notoriety, but plenty of bandits are robbers who try
not to kill - partly to avoid a death sentence, and partly because a thief is
not automatically a killer. None of that stops them shooting in self-defence
against the law, or taking hostages to protect their own.

## Gangs

There is said to be no honour among thieves, and yet many bandit gangs are
small, close-knit and fiercely loyal. For many the gang stands in for family,
and among D-bees especially it often is family - brothers and cousins can make
up a quarter to two-thirds of a band. Such gangs arrange jailbreaks, take
hostages to swap for imprisoned members, and carry vendettas against particular
lawmen, towns, tribes and rival gangs.

Leadership goes to the strongest, but in a gang the strongest is whoever holds
the respect of the most people - which is why a cunning or charismatic bandit
leads more often than a brute with a P.S. of 32 and an I.Q. of 7.

## Lore

"Bandit" is a broad term rather than a single trade. Any selfish or evil
character who robs, kidnaps, extorts or murders can be called one, and most
real outlaws are professional criminals or men of arms - soldiers, mercenaries,
Juicers, Crazies and ''Borgs - who turned to armed robbery. Psychics,
practitioners of magic and supernatural beings ride with gangs too, but they
are only about five percent of the outlaw population around the Pecos Empire.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'bandit');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'bandit';

